From u63023c771a070260 at groups.google.com  Wed Apr 15 21:36:25 2015
From: u63023c771a070260 at groups.google.com (Antonio Piccolboni)
Date: Wed, 15 Apr 2015 21:36:25 +0000
Subject: Re: RHadoop: Using more mappers on large files
Message-ID: <X4YDTCOveqc.DpStQkxNyawJ@rhadoop.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <X4YDTCOveqc.gGQVqmvz0okJ@rhadoop.groups.google.com>
References: <X4YDTCOveqc.gGQVqmvz0okJ@rhadoop.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rhadoop/c/X4YDTCOveqc/m/DpStQkxNyawJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Please look into the vectorized.reduce argument to mapreduce. That requires you to also write a vectorized reducer, that is a reducer that can process multiple reduce groups in one call (in this case, multiple words). Writing an efficient one can be tricky. If I were doing this, I would form a data frame with keys and values than use dplyr to aggregate it (functions group and summarise).

Antonio

On Tuesday, March 31, 2015 at 11:54:49 PM UTC-7, LAKSHMI PRAKASH wrote:
> I am trying to use RHadoop to run word count on a 50GB file. It works with 513 mappers and 142 reducers but takes 37 mins! I was trying to reduce the time by increasing the number of mappers to more than 1000, but it doesn't work. Any ideas why this is so?
> Thanks!

